{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VCV8iqTY8BMn3gM4XKuTWP","type":"tool_result","content":"# ACL Rolling Review Form - Complete Scoring Rubric\n\n## Reviewer Confidence\n- **5**: \"Positive that my evaluation is correct. I read the paper very carefully and am familiar with related work.\"\n- **4**: \"Quite sure. I tried to check the important points carefully.\"\n- **3**: \"Pretty sure, but there's a chance I missed something.\"\n- **2**: \"Willing to defend my evaluation, but it is fairly likely that I missed some details.\"\n- **1**: \"Not my area, or paper is very hard to understand.\"\n\n## Soundness\n- **5**: \"Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen, given its type.\"\n- **4**: \"Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims.\"\n- **3**: \"Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims.\"\n- **2**: \"Poor: Some of the main claims are not sufficiently supported.\"\n- **1**: \"Major Issues: This study is not yet sufficiently thorough to warrant publication.\"\n\n## Excitement\n- **5**: \"Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation.\"\n- **4**: \"Exciting: I would mention this paper to others and/or make an effort to attend.\"\n- **3**: \"Interesting: I might mention some points of this paper to others.\"\n- **2**: \"Potentially Interesting: this paper does not resonate with me, but it might with others.\"\n- **1**: \"Not Exciting: this paper does not resonate with me.\"\n\n## Overall Assessment\n- **5**: Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- **4.5**: Borderline Award\n- **4**: Conference acceptance\n- **3.5**: Borderline Conference\n- **3**: Findings acceptance\n- **2.5**: Borderline Findings\n- **2**: Resubmit next cycle\n- **1.5**: Resubmit after next cycle\n- **1**: Do not resubmit\n\n## Reproducibility\n- **5**: \"They could easily reproduce the results.\"\n- **4**: \"They could mostly reproduce the results.\"\n- **3**: \"They could reproduce the results with some difficulty.\"\n- **2**: \"They would be hard pressed to reproduce the results.\"\n- **1**: \"They would not be able to reproduce the results.\"\n\n## Datasets & Software\nBoth use identical 1-5 scales ranging from \"Enabling\" to \"No usable [datasets/software] submitted.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f25cfed-835a-4517-9646-6292208db176","uuid":"e9109eea-d4ea-44d5-8c18-ba684a24f947","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:08:16.182Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":25365,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# ACL Rolling Review Form - Complete Scoring Rubric\n\n## Reviewer Confidence\n- **5**: \"Positive that my evaluation is correct. I read the paper very carefully and am familiar with related work.\"\n- **4**: \"Quite sure. I tried to check the important points carefully.\"\n- **3**: \"Pretty sure, but there's a chance I missed something.\"\n- **2**: \"Willing to defend my evaluation, but it is fairly likely that I missed some details.\"\n- **1**: \"Not my area, or paper is very hard to understand.\"\n\n## Soundness\n- **5**: \"Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen, given its type.\"\n- **4**: \"Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims.\"\n- **3**: \"Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims.\"\n- **2**: \"Poor: Some of the main claims are not sufficiently supported.\"\n- **1**: \"Major Issues: This study is not yet sufficiently thorough to warrant publication.\"\n\n## Excitement\n- **5**: \"Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation.\"\n- **4**: \"Exciting: I would mention this paper to others and/or make an effort to attend.\"\n- **3**: \"Interesting: I might mention some points of this paper to others.\"\n- **2**: \"Potentially Interesting: this paper does not resonate with me, but it might with others.\"\n- **1**: \"Not Exciting: this paper does not resonate with me.\"\n\n## Overall Assessment\n- **5**: Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- **4.5**: Borderline Award\n- **4**: Conference acceptance\n- **3.5**: Borderline Conference\n- **3**: Findings acceptance\n- **2.5**: Borderline Findings\n- **2**: Resubmit next cycle\n- **1.5**: Resubmit after next cycle\n- **1**: Do not resubmit\n\n## Reproducibility\n- **5**: \"They could easily reproduce the results.\"\n- **4**: \"They could mostly reproduce the results.\"\n- **3**: \"They could reproduce the results with some difficulty.\"\n- **2**: \"They would be hard pressed to reproduce the results.\"\n- **1**: \"They would not be able to reproduce the results.\"\n\n## Datasets & Software\nBoth use identical 1-5 scales ranging from \"Enabling\" to \"No usable [datasets/software] submitted.\"","durationMs":6819,"url":"https://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019XtkprzzZA8Vpp8r9pdHnr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the complete ARR review form with scoring scales. Let me also get EMNLP acceptance rate data.\n\nNow I have all the information I need:\n\n**ARR/EMNLP Review Form:**\n- Soundness: 1-5 scale\n- Excitement: 1-5 scale\n- Overall Assessment: 1-5 scale (with 0.5 increments)\n- Reproducibility: 1-5 scale\n- Confidence: 1-5 scale\n\n**EMNLP acceptance rate:** ~25% (typical for recent years)\n\n\nI'm going to dispatch two independent reviewers in parallel—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—making sure they're completely blind to the paper's tier. After they finish, I'll run the roundtable discussion. Let me prepare detailed review prompts with the paper summary and evaluation criteria for both of them.\n\nThe paper is FREIGE, which proposes the first LLM-based document-level relation extraction system that jointly generates relation triples and sentence-level evidence. The key innovation is a contrastive evidence discrimination scoring function that outperforms standard NLI approaches, and the authors show that their supervised fine-tuning approach avoids the catastrophic forgetting problem that occurs with GRPO+QLoRA. On the DocRED benchmark, they achieve 0.455 relation F1 and 0.376 evidence F1, with a 5.5 percentage point improvement over the SFT baseline, and they validate their EDCR metric through human annotation. Now I need to review the experimental setup details from the context file. Looking at what actually made it into the paper, I can see most of the planned experiments are represented across the tables—the core ablations on CED versus flat-NLI, the RL spectrum comparisons, the EDCR diagnostics and human validation, and the model size studies all appear. Table 9 seems to be cut off, so I'm not sure what that final experiment covers yet.\n\nNow I'm moving on to set up the reviewer assignments, dispatching the methods and experiments reviewer alongside someone focused on novelty and related work positioning.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":714,"cache_read_input_tokens":73134,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":714},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f25cfed-835a-4517-9646-6292208db176","uuid":"ed679377-5d98-4772-a51f-80856f3403db"}
