{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019XtkprzzZA8Vpp8r9pdHnr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NP9Y11DQpg1pHPZ263S9Gi","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. Produce a blind, independent review. Do NOT speculate about whether the paper will be accepted or rejected — just evaluate objectively.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ACL Rolling Review)\n\n### Scoring scales:\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent (most thorough), 4=Strong (sufficient for all claims), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Poor (main claims not supported), 1=Major issues\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Potentially interesting, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5, 0.5 increments): 5=Award, 4.5=Borderline award, 4=Conference accept, 3.5=Borderline conference, 3=Findings, 2.5=Borderline findings, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1.5=Resubmit after next, 1=Do not resubmit\n- **Reproducibility** (1-5): 5=Easily reproduce, 4=Mostly reproduce, 3=With some difficulty, 2=Hard pressed, 1=Cannot reproduce\n- **Confidence** (1-5): 5=Positive evaluation correct, 4=Quite sure, 3=Pretty sure, 2=Fairly likely missed details, 1=Not my area\n\n### EMNLP typical acceptance rate: ~25% main conference, ~40% including Findings\n\n---\n\n## Paper: FREIGE: Faithful Evidence Generation for LLM-Based Document-Level Relation Extraction via Contrastive Evidence Discrimination\n\n### Abstract\nLLMs are closing the gap with encoder-based methods on document-level relation extraction (DocRE), yet no existing LLM approach produces verifiable evidence. FREIGE is the first generative LLM framework jointly outputting relation triples and explicit sentence-level evidence citations on DocRED. Core component: CED (Contrastive Evidence Discrimination), a scoring function measuring evidence quality via the entailment gap between gold evidence and best entity-sharing distractor. Key findings: (1) naïve NLI scoring fooled by entity-sharing distractors; (2) online RL (GRPO+QLoRA) with CED reward → catastrophic forgetting (evidence–extraction decoupling: Evi-F1 up to 0.850 while rel_F1 < 0.02); (3) CED as offline quality filter for RSFT yields +5.5pp rel_F1, +1.7pp Evi-F1 cond, −7.1pp EDCR on DocRED test over SFT. Also proposes EDCR metric (human validated, Pearson r=0.62).\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n**Task:** Given document D with sentences {S1,...,Sn} and entity mentions, output tuples (h, r, t, E) where E ⊆ {S1,...,Sn} is evidence.\n\n**CED (Contrastive Evidence Discrimination):**\n- flat-NLI(E) = (1/|E|) Σ_{s∈E} p_entail(s | h,r,t) — vulnerable to entity-sharing distractors\n- CED(E,D) = (1/|E|) Σ_{s∈E} [p_entail(s) − max_{s'∈N(h,t,D)} p_entail(s')]\n- N(h,t,D) = {s' ∈ D\\E_gold : s' mentions h or t} — intra-document hard negatives\n- Implemented with DeBERTa-v3-base fine-tuned on MNLI (86M params)\n\n**CED-Guided RSFT (3 stages):**\n1. SFT: Qwen3-4B + QLoRA (rank=64) on gold DocRED (12,151 examples)\n2. Candidate Generation: N=8 candidates per doc at temp=0.7, score with CED, keep top-k=1 → ~2,718 samples (12.4% retention)\n3. RSFT: Fine-tune SFT model on CED-filtered candidates with standard supervised loss\n\n**EDCR metric:** EDCR = |{s ∈ Ê : s ∉ E_gold ∧ s ∈ N(h,t,D)}| / |Ê|\n\n### Experiments (Section 4)\n\n**Setup:** DocRED + Re-DocRED; Qwen3-4B base; metrics: rel_F1, Ign_F1, Evi-F1_joint (DREEAM protocol), Evi-F1_cond, EDCR↓\n\n**Table 1 — Test set results:**\n| Method | Type | rel_F1 | Ign_F1 | Evi-F1_J | Evi-F1_C | EDCR↓ |\n|--------|------|--------|--------|----------|----------|-------|\n| DREEAM | Enc. | — | — | 0.573 | 0.834 | — |\n| SAIS | Enc. | — | — | 0.557 | — | — |\n| LMRC | Gen. | 0.607 | — | — | — | — |\n| SFT baseline | Gen. | 0.400 | 0.381 | 0.322 | 0.790 | 0.719 |\n| RSFT flat-NLI | Gen. | 0.430 | 0.412 | 0.342 | 0.788 | 0.701 |\n| FREIGE | Gen. | 0.455 | 0.437 | 0.376 | 0.807 | 0.648 |\n\n**Table 3 — Online-offline RL spectrum (dev):**\n| Method | RL Type | rel_F1 | Evi-F1 | EDCR↓ | Catastrophic? |\n|--------|---------|--------|--------|-------|---------------|\n| GRPO G=4 | On-policy | 0.014 | 0.850 | 0.988 | Yes |\n| GRPO G=8 | On-policy | 0.031 | 0.816 | 0.981 | Yes |\n| DPO+CED | Offline pref | 0.325 | 0.786 | 0.683 | No (degraded) |\n| SFT | No RL | 0.406 | 0.798 | 0.712 | No |\n| RSFT+CED | Offline filtered | 0.468 | 0.816 | 0.639 | No |\n\n**Table 4 — CED vs flat-NLI ablation (dev):**\nCED RSFT: rel_F1=0.468, Evi-F1=0.816, EDCR=0.639 (2,718 samples)\nflat-NLI RSFT best: rel_F1=0.441, Evi-F1=0.797, EDCR=0.689 (2,845 samples)\nΔ = +2.71pp rel_F1, +2.08pp Evi-F1, −5.64pp EDCR (all p<0.01)\n\n**Table 5 — CED usage modes:** CED-reranker (inference): lowest EDCR (0.598) but modest rel_F1 gain (+0.95pp). RSFT CED (training): +6.26pp rel_F1. Confirms CED best as training signal, not inference reranker.\n\n**Table 6 — Cross-method EDCR diagnostic:** Clear gradient from GRPO (0.988) to CED-reranker (0.598). Pearson r between EDCR and Evi-F1 = −0.42.\n\n**Table 7 — Human EDCR validation:** 100 samples, 3 annotators, κ=0.72. Pearson r=0.62 between auto EDCR and human distractor rate. Method ranking preserved.\n\n**Table 8 — Model size ablation:** 1.7B (+3.12pp), 4B (+6.26pp), 8B (+4.89pp) — consistent gains.\n\n**Table 9 — Re-DocRED:** RSFT+CED delta preserved (+5.55pp vs +6.26pp on DocRED).\n\n**Table 10 — top_k ablation:** k=1 (2,718 samples) > k=3 (7,856 samples) across all metrics.\n\n**Table 11 — NLI model ablation:** DeBERTa-base vs large: +0.38pp rel_F1, marginal.\n\n**Table 12 — Evidence baseline comparison (dev):** FREIGE rel_F1=0.468, Evi-F1_J=0.381; DREEAM rel_F1=0.631, Evi-F1_J=0.572. Gap driven by extraction, not evidence quality.\n\n### Experimental Design Reference (complete planned experiment list):\n13 experiments were planned:\n1. CED vs flat-NLI RSFT ablation ✓ (Table 4)\n2. Test set evaluation ✓ (Table 1)\n3. EDCR cross-method diagnostic ✓ (Table 6)\n4. DREEAM baseline reproduction ✓ (Table 12)\n5. CED-as-reranker PoC ✓ (Table 5)\n6. Model size ablation (1.7B/8B) ✓ (Table 8)\n7. Re-DocRED extension ✓ (Table 9)\n8. top_k ablation ✓ (Table 10)\n9. GRPO forgetting analysis + visualization ✓ (described in §4.3, but no figure in paper)\n10. NLI model ablation ✓ (Table 11)\n11. GRPO G=8 experiment ✓ (Table 3)\n12. DPO+CED experiment ✓ (Table 3)\n13. EDCR human validation ✓ (Table 7)\n14. Evi-F1 DREEAM protocol alignment ✓ (Table 2)\n\nCoverage: 14/14 experiments are reflected in the paper.\n\n---\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nFocus on **Methods** and **Experiments**. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is CED well-motivated? Is the formulation correct? Are there flaws in the RSFT pipeline? Is EDCR well-defined?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are ablations sufficient and fair? Are baselines appropriate? Are statistical tests proper? Is the setup reproducible?\n3. **Claims vs evidence**: Does each claim have sufficient experimental support? Are there overclaims?\n4. **Missing experiments or controls**: What's missing that would strengthen the paper?\n5. **Clarity of experimental setup**: Are hyperparameters, training details, evaluation protocols clearly described?\n\n## Output Format\n\nProduce your review in this exact format:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentence summary of the paper's contribution]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- Q2: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Missing Experiments / Suggestions\n- M1: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X.X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n\n### Justification for Overall Score\n[2-3 sentences]\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":714,"cache_read_input_tokens":73134,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":714},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f25cfed-835a-4517-9646-6292208db176","uuid":"60e01e43-f4fb-4d0f-bf27-8ec368863c89"}
